When you run into `@@@` comments, you need to check if the condition is `true` or `false`. For example, 

<code>
    Debug.Assert(!(OLD(_items.Length != _defaultCapacity || OLD(Capacity) != _defaultCapacity));
</code>

This code will raise an `AssertionException` if the length of `_items` does not equals to `_defaultCapacity` or the `Capacity` is not equals to `_defaultCapacity`.

Please note that `OLD` refers to the value of the variable at its declaration point which may not be equal to the original value in the current invocation of the function. This also applies to `RET`, referring to the return value of a function.

So, the output for your given C# code will vary according to how you interpret the comment (`Value of x increases by y in the function`). You may need to modify the code to match the actual behavior as written.

